<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      A permission is declared by the module that needs it, not by :app.

      Announcing that updates exist. Since Android 13 this is a runtime permission, asked for on
      first real use: here that is the first "updates available" notification, which comes from a
      worker. A worker cannot request a permission — it has no UI — so the request stays where the
      user is in the foreground, and a denied notification simply is not seen. The periodic check
      keeps running and the Home list stays correct: the notification is a notice, not the mechanism.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      NO `RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED`, and that is not an oversight.

      It would be for "rescheduling the workers". WorkManager reschedules its own periodic work after
      a reboot: the library declares **its own** boot receiver and rebuilds the queue from its own
      database. Adding the permission here would mean asking for one we do not use, for a receiver
      that does not exist.
    -->

</manifest>
